gajU(){return"Bunch"},
gajV(){return"For plantain. Not weighed."},
gajM(){return"Crate"},
gajN(){return"For fruits and vegetables. Weight varies."},
gahP(){return"Case A \u2014 coffee in production (2 ha, 3000 plants)"},
gahL(){return"Coffee, Producing, Perennial, 2 ha, 3000 plants"},
gahM(){return"COP 500,000 expense on Fertilizer"},
gahN(){return"COP 750,000 income from selling 50 kg (4 arrobas)"},
gahO(){return"Balance +COP 250,000 \xb7 Margin 33% \xb7 ROI 50%"},
gahZ(){return"Case B \u2014 freshly planted plantain (500 plants on 0.5 ha)"},
gahV(){return"Plantain, Not producing yet, Perennial, 0.5 ha, 500 plants"},
gahW(){return"Initial planting, 500 plants, 0.5 ha, cost COP 200,000"},
gahX(){return"COP 100,000 labor and COP 150,000 fertilizer"},
gahY(){return"Investment COP 450,000. It does not say loss: it is an investment until it produces."},
gaj9(){return"And per plant? When does it start yielding?"},
gaj7(){return"If you know how many live plants the crop has, the app also shows kilos per plant. It helps when you counted the plants but did not measure the land."},
gaj8(){return"Next to it you get your own average from previous years. It is nobody's table: the app measures against your own land."},
gaj6(){return"If you wrote when you expected the first harvest and that date passed with no harvest, the app warns you: \u201cshould be yielding since \u2026\u201d."},
gaiN(){return"The rest of what the app does"},
gajE(){return"Your plots (terrenos)"},
gajA(){return"A plot is the land where your crop sits. It does two things: keeps the plot's location and splits one expense that belongs to several crops together."},
gajF(){return"In Crops, tap the mountain icon to create the plot: name, hectares if you measured them, which crops go inside, and the location if you want."},
gajB(){return"You can take the location from the phone, type it by hand, or leave it empty. The app never re-measures it on its own."},
gajD(){return"An expense for the whole plot (a clearing, the fertilizer) is logged on the plot and split by hectares. If a crop has no area, that part is not split and shows separately."},
gajC(){return"In Report a \u201cYour plots \xb7 location\u201d card appears only if some plot has a coordinate."},
gak5(){return"Workers and day wages"},
gak2(){return"You save your workers so you don't type their pay each time."},
gak6(){return"In the \u22ee menu \u2192 Workers. Give a name and, if you want, their pay per day; if you leave it empty, you write it when logging each day wage."},
gak3(){return"The day wage is logged as a labor expense in Record: you pick the worker and the days. The name is saved with the record, so deleting a worker does not delete their history."},
gak4(){return"In Report you see the period's payroll (who, how many days, the total) and the monthly timesheet."},
gahE(){return"The month's cash box"},
gahD(){return"The cash box is the money you set aside each month for the farm: it covers day wages and small expenses (power, water, repairs)."},
gahF(){return"You set it in Settings \u2192 \u201cMonthly cash contribution\u201d. If you leave it empty, the card does not show."},
gahC(){return"What you don't spend is not lost: it carries over to the next month. The bar turns green, amber, or red depending on how much you have spent."},
gaje(){return"The report and its files"},
gajd(){return"In Report you pick the period (week, month, year, or year-to-date) and see whether you made or lost money, the cost per kilo, how each crop is doing, the month's cash box, and what is worth doing next."},
gajc(){return"With the export buttons you take it as PDF or Excel, plus a balance template. Share it wherever you want."},
gahz(){return"Your backup"},
gahy(){return"A backup is a file of your own with all your data: crops, plantings, expenses, sales, workers, and settings."},
gahB(){return"In Settings \u2192 Backup you export it (and share it) and restore it whenever you want."},
gahx(){return"Restoring deletes nothing: what you already have stays and the file's data is added."},
gahA(){return"This is not the same as syncing. Syncing saves to the cloud so you can see it from another device."},
gaiM(){return"Language and how numbers are read"},
gaiK(){return"The app speaks Spanish and English: you pick it in Settings \u2192 \u201cLanguage\u201d."},
gaiL(){return"Numbers are read the way your language does: in Spanish COP 1.500 and in English COP 1,500."},
gaiJ(){return"The currency is picked once and always read with its code (COP), never with a symbol."},
gaiH(){return"Glossary"},
gaiF(){return"Seedlings: young plantation in establishment. Area: crop surface. Live plants: current total (planting \u2212 losses + replanting). Sold vs harvested: how much of the harvest has already gone out as a sale. Sync: saves to the cloud to log in from any device. Alert: automatic notice on the Overview."},
gaiG(){return"View financial glossary"},
gaj_(){return"What does per-hectare mean?"},
gaiX(){return"Normalizing per hectare makes your data comparable: you can tell whether one plot outperforms another even when their sizes differ."},
gaj3(){return"Yield (kg/ha)"},
gaj5(){return"Harvests in the period \xf7 crop area. The same standard as your exports."},
gaj2(){return"Sales per ha"},
